--- !u!114 &-5802939168721803928
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "Volumes allow you to overrides certain HDRP settings per Camera, this includes post-processing effects, lighting, shadows, fog, sky, among others. These property overrides are stored in assets on disk called <b>Volume Profiles.</b>\n\nThere are 3 main types of Volumes:  <b>Default,</b> <b>Global,</b> and <b>Local.</b>\n\n\u2022 The <b>Default Volume</b> lives under <b>Edit</b> > <b>Project Settings</b> > <b>Graphics</b> > <b>HDRP Global Settings.</b> It defines the state of all project-wide Volume properties affecting every Camera, in every Scene. Therefore, it is useful for setting properties that are consistent across your project, such as Tonemapping and Motion Blur, regardless of the loaded Scenes and the active Quality Level and Render Pipeline Asset.\n\n\u2022 A <b>Global Volume</b> lives inside the Scene as a Volume GameObject component. It can be considered as infinitely large and is useful to control properties affecting the entire world, such as Sky, Fog and Shadows, regardless of the Camera position.\n\n\u2022 A <b>Local Volume</b> also lives inside the Scene as a Volume GameObject component, however it is used to override Volume properties within a very specific area of the scene via a Box, Sphere, Capsule or Mesh collider. Typically, the overriddes will be related to Post-processing and Exposure, as these can greatly vary depending on the position of the Camera in the Scene.\n\n\n<b>Expert tips</b>\n\nA fourth type of Volumes exists under <b>Edit</b> > <b>Project Settings</b> > <b>Quality</b> > <b>HDRP</b> > <b>Volumes.</b> This <b>Quality Volume</b> lets you define properties that are specific to a Render Pipeline Asset, and therefore it can be tied to a given Quality level and/or Platform. This may be useful for settings up the properties of more expensive effects, such as the Ray Tracing ones.\n\nThe properties set in the <b>Default Volume</b> and the <b>Quality Volume</b> are applied at load time, therefore they do not have a runtime cost associated with them. In contrast, Volume overrides defined using <b>Global</b> and <b>Local Volumes</b> inside the scenes are evaluated every frame. Therefore, it is recommended to use the <b>Default Volume</b> and optionally the <b>Quality Volume</b> for values that do not need to vary at runtime."
--- !u!114 &-5428121327714997408
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "In this scene, there are 6 <b>Local</b> Volumes used to control HDRP effects in specific rooms.\n\n1. Enable Gizmos in order to see the boundaries of <b>Local</b> volumes.\n\n2. Select <b>Volume Room 3</b> and turn it on and off. \n\nNotice that this Volume fully encompass the 3rd room and it has overrides for Exposure, White Balance and Fog."
