--- !u!114 &6504860795873735246
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "Next, let\u2019s look at the Global Volume properties.\n\n1. In the <i>Hierarchy,</i> expand <b>Lighting</b> > <b>Volumes.</b>\n\n2. Select the <b>Volume Global</b> GameObject.\n\n3. Notice the <b>Mode</b> is <b>Global</b> in the <i>Inspector.</i>\n\n4. Disable the <b>HDRI Sky</b> Override. You can see that the sky disappears.\n\n5. Undo your change.\n\n<b>Global Volumes</b> have an effect on cameras wherever they are located in the scene. They are convenient for defining values that remain constant thoughtout the scene, for instance:\n\\u2022 Sky\n\\u2022 Fog\n\\u2022 Shadows\n"
