--- !u!114 &7239507449268867957
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "In this tutorial, you learned how Default, Global and Local Volumes can be used to override the HDRP effects based on the camera position.\n\nYou can also watch  a<a href=\"https://youtu.be/yqCHiZrgKzs?t=771\">video</a> tutorial to learn more about the Volume framework.\n\nFor more details, check out the corresponding documentation:\n\u2022<a href=\"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.render-pipelines.high-definition@latest/index.html?subfolder=/manual/understand-volumes.html\">Volume Framework</a>\n\u2022<a href=\"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.render-pipelines.high-definition@latest/index.html?subfolder=//manual/use-the-rendering-debugger.html\">Volume Debugging</a>\n\n<b>Note:</b> You do not need to save your changes in order to follow other tutorials."
--- !u!114 &7397739669709159401
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "Let\u2019s see how the Default Volume properties are defined.\n\n1. Under <b>Edit</b> > <b>Project Settings</b> > <b>Graphics,</b> select <b>HDRP Global Settings.</b> \n\n2. In the <b>Default Volume Profile</b> section, search for the <b>Color Adjustments</b> component, using the dedicated search bar.\n\n3. Expand the component, and move the <b>Saturation</b> sllider left and right. \n\nYou can see that the entire Scene becomes either less or more saturated, regardless of the camera position.\n\n4. Undo your change by resetting the <b>Saturation</b> to 0. Alternatively, you may right click on the property to revert it to its default value."
